# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Session 9 interview bank: the WHOLE course, grouped by theme in course order.

Built 2026-11-14 for the last class (OUTLINE.md part PQ). Each item is
{q, a, level, skill}; `skill` may be any skill in skills.js (sessions 0-9),
and the session page links a skill from another session to that session's
page. Themes, in order: the machine and the market (W0), objects (W1),
concurrency (W2), the wire (W3), backtesting and data (W4), complexity (W6),
big data (W7), machine learning (W8), testing / git flow / CI/CD (W9),
market structure, risk and the season, and system design.

Never names a student; never describes the Week 9 hidden signal.
"""


def Q(level, skill, q, a):
    return {"q": q, "a": a, "level": level, "skill": skill}


INTERVIEW = [
    # ---------------- Week 0: the machine and the market ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "trading.clob",
      "Week 0 · A book shows 99.98 bid and 100.02 ask. What are the mid and the spread in basis points, and what does buying 100 shares at market cost you here?",
      "The mid is 100.00 and the spread is 0.04, which is 4 bp of the mid. A market buy lifts the ask, so you pay half the spread (2 bp) relative to the mid, plus the taker fee — 15 bp of notional on this venue's default schedule, about $15 on $10,002. The lesson of Week 0 is that on many venues the fee is bigger than a tight spread, so trading immediately always costs something and a strategy has to clear both."),
    Q("warm-up", "quant.five-pillars",
      "Week 0 · Name the five pillars of a systematic trading system and say which one most student bots neglect.",
      "Signal (what you believe), strategy (what you want to hold given the belief), execution (how you get there at what cost), risk (what stops you losing too much) and portfolio (cash, positions, P&L across everything). Most first bots are all signal: they emit an order whenever the indicator fires, with no sizing, no cost model and no limit, so execution and risk are where they bleed. The pillars also map onto the code: the signal is a pure function, risk is a veto in front of the wire, the portfolio is reconciled against the venue."),
    Q("core", "trading.market-making",
      "Week 0 · Why does a market maker quote a spread at all? Name its three costs.",
      "The spread pays for order processing (fees, infrastructure, capital), inventory risk (after a fill the maker holds a position the market can move against) and adverse selection (the counterparty who lifts you may know something you do not). When any of the three rises — volatility, a large one-sided position, toxic flow — the maker widens or skews its quotes. That is exactly the logic of the broker role in the arena: vol-adjusted spread, inventory skew, toxic-flow detection."),
    Q("core", "trading.order-types",
      "Week 0 · A limit order or a market order: what does each buy you and what does it cost you?",
      "A market order buys certainty of fill and pays with uncertainty of price: it walks the book until filled and pays the taker fee. A limit order buys certainty of price and pays with uncertainty of fill: it may rest and never trade, or trade only when the price has moved against you (adverse selection), but it earns the maker rebate. Marketable limit orders and IOC sit between them, which is why execution logic is a choice per situation rather than a default."),
    Q("senior", "tools.latency-tail",
      "Week 0 · Where does the time go between a market-data byte arriving and your order leaving, and why is Python fine in this course but not in a colocated HFT engine?",
      "The time goes in the network (a round trip over the internet is milliseconds, inside a colocated rack microseconds), the kernel and the socket layer, deserialisation, your decision and the send. Inside the process the costs are memory and cache misses (tens to hundreds of nanoseconds each), allocation and garbage collection, and the interpreter itself. The arena ticks every 0.5 s and the network dominates, so Python's overhead is irrelevant and its productivity wins; when the budget is a few microseconds end to end, you need C++ or an FPGA, preallocated memory, kernel bypass and you measure the p99, not the mean."),

    # ---------------- Week 1: objects ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "python.inheritance",
      "Week 1 · Inheritance or composition: how do you choose, and give one example of each from a trading system?",
      "Inheritance expresses IS-A and shares a contract: a MomentumTrader is a Trader, so it subclasses arena.Trader and overrides on_tick. Composition expresses HAS-A and shares behaviour without coupling the types: a Trader has a RiskManager and a Portfolio, and you can swap either without touching the class hierarchy. Prefer composition by default, because deep hierarchies make one change ripple through every subclass; keep inheritance for the narrow, stable interfaces the engine calls."),
    Q("core", "python.abc",
      "Week 1 · Your bot never calls the engine's main loop; the engine calls your on_tick. What is that design, and what does an abstract base class add to it?",
      "It is inversion of control, the template-method idea: the framework owns the loop, the sockets and the accounting, and calls the hooks you override, so the same subclass runs in the offline simulator and live. Polymorphism is what makes that work — the engine calls on_tick on whatever Trader it was given. An ABC makes the contract explicit and checked early: a subclass that forgets an @abstractmethod cannot even be instantiated, so the mistake surfaces at construction, not hours into a session."),
    Q("core", "python.dataclasses",
      "Week 1 · When do you use a @dataclass and when a Pydantic model?",
      "Choose by where the data comes from. A dataclass is a light value object for data your own code creates and trusts — an internal Order, a Fill record — with equality and a repr for free, frozen if it should not change. A Pydantic model validates and coerces at a trust boundary — anything that arrives over the wire or from a file — and gives JSON in and out, which is why every arena message is a Pydantic model in shared/messages.py. Validating internal objects on every construction wastes time; trusting external ones is how junk reaches your book."),
    Q("core", "python.strategy-pattern",
      "Week 1 · How does the plugin registry (register_security, register_strategy, register_shock) follow the strategy pattern, and what does it buy the platform?",
      "Each plugin is a function with a fixed signature — a security is (prev_price, tick) -> price, a strategy returns a Signal or None — and the engine stores them in a registry and calls whatever is registered at the right moment. That is the strategy pattern: the algorithm varies behind one interface, so adding a security or a shock needs no change to the engine, which is the open/closed principle. The engine also wraps every call in try/except, so a plugin can fail without taking the venue down."),
    Q("senior", "python.classes",
      "Week 1 · A colleague passes orders around as plain dicts. Why is a dict not a design?",
      "A dict has no place to keep an invariant: nothing stops a negative quantity, a side spelled 'Buy', or a typo'd key that silently reads as missing. Every consumer has to re-validate, and every change to the shape is a search through the code base. A small class or a frozen dataclass checks the invariant once where the object is built, documents the fields with types, and lets a tool find every use; at the wire boundary a Pydantic model does the same with validation. A dict is fine as a transport format, not as the domain model."),

    # ---------------- Week 2: concurrency ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "python.gil",
      "Week 2 · What is the difference between concurrency and parallelism, and what does the GIL change in Python?",
      "Concurrency is dealing with many things at once (interleaving waits); parallelism is doing many things at the same instant on several cores. The GIL lets only one thread execute Python bytecode at a time, so threads give concurrency for I/O-bound work (they release the GIL while waiting) but no speed-up for CPU-bound Python. CPU-bound work needs processes, or native code that releases the GIL, such as most of numpy."),
    Q("core", "python.non-blocking",
      "Week 2 · Your bot stays connected but stops reacting for seconds at a time. on_tick calls requests.get(). Explain and fix.",
      "on_tick runs on the event loop, and requests.get is a blocking call: while it waits, the loop cannot run the task that reads the socket, so no snapshot, ack or fill is processed. The bot does not crash — it goes quiet while still looking connected. Fix it by moving the call off the loop with await asyncio.to_thread(...), or by using an async client, or better by doing the fetch in a separate task that updates a cache which on_tick only reads."),
    Q("core", "python.to-thread",
      "Week 2 · Why does the broker poll yfinance on a thread when everything else in the platform is asyncio?",
      "yfinance is blocking HTTP code that you cannot rewrite, and a blocking call on the event loop would freeze the broker's quoting and its socket. Running the poll on a thread keeps the loop free: the thread spends almost all its time waiting on the network, which releases the GIL, and it hands each new price to the loop through a thread-safe handoff. It is the one sanctioned exception to the platform's asyncio discipline, and the reason is precisely that the work is I/O-bound and un-awaitable."),
    Q("core", "python.async-races",
      "Week 2 · asyncio is single-threaded, so how can two coroutines still race on your position?",
      "Each await is a point where the loop may run another task. If a coroutine reads the position, awaits a send, and then writes position + qty, another coroutine can update the position in between, and one update is lost — a check-then-act race across an await. The fixes are to keep the read-modify-write free of awaits, to guard it with an asyncio.Lock, or to give the state a single owner that receives updates through a queue."),
    Q("core", "python.asyncio-queue",
      "Week 2 · Why give an asyncio.Queue a maxsize between the feed reader and the strategy?",
      "An unbounded queue turns a consumer that is slower than the feed into a growing backlog: memory rises, and worse, the strategy trades on data that is seconds old while believing it is current. A bounded queue makes put() wait when the queue is full, which is backpressure: the producer slows down to the consumer's pace. For market data you often go further and coalesce — keep only the latest snapshot per symbol — because a stale snapshot has no value."),
    Q("senior", "python.concurrency-choice",
      "Week 2 · Pick threads, processes or asyncio for each: the venue socket, a 500-point parameter sweep, a yfinance poll, and writing the tape to disk.",
      "The venue socket is I/O-bound with many small events, so asyncio. The parameter sweep is CPU-bound and embarrassingly parallel, so a process pool with picklable functions and small arguments, behind an if __name__ == '__main__' guard. The yfinance poll is blocking I/O you cannot make async, so one thread or asyncio.to_thread. Writing the tape is blocking file I/O that must not stall the loop: a writer thread fed by a queue, or batched writes handed off with to_thread."),

    # ---------------- Week 3: the wire ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "tools.udp-tcp",
      "Week 3 · Why do real venues publish market data over UDP multicast but take orders over TCP?",
      "Market data goes to thousands of receivers at once and newer data replaces older, so multicast UDP is efficient and a lost packet is handled by sequence numbers and a recovery channel rather than by slowing everyone down. Orders must arrive exactly once and in order, and the sender must know they arrived, so they go over TCP with acknowledgements. The arena simplifies both onto one WebSocket over TCP, which is why its snapshots can queue behind each other when a reader is slow."),
    Q("core", "python.socket-examples",
      "Week 3 · Your TCP reader sometimes parses half a message, or two glued together. What is wrong?",
      "TCP is a byte stream, not a message stream: one recv() can return part of a message or several messages. The reader must put the boundaries back itself — with a delimiter such as a newline, or a length prefix read first — by accumulating bytes in a buffer and extracting only complete frames. WebSockets do this framing for you, which is one reason the arena uses them; a raw socket protocol must always define its framing explicitly."),
    Q("core", "trading.fix-itch",
      "Week 3 · What are sequence numbers for on a market-data feed, and what do you do when you see a gap?",
      "Every message carries an increasing sequence number so a receiver can prove it saw everything. A gap means messages were lost: the book you hold may now be wrong, so you must stop trusting it, request a retransmission or a snapshot from the recovery channel, and rebuild before trading again. Quietly continuing after a gap is how a bot ends up quoting against an order that was cancelled long ago."),
    Q("core", "python.reconnect",
      "Week 3 · Write the rules of a reconnect loop for a trading client in a few sentences.",
      "On a close, wait and retry with capped exponential backoff plus random jitter, so a thousand clients do not hammer a restarted venue at the same instant. After reconnecting, authenticate again and resynchronise: clear the stale book, take a fresh snapshot, and adopt the venue's positions rather than your local ones. Never resend an order blindly — retries need a client order id so a duplicate is a no-op. And log each attempt, because a silent reconnect loop looks exactly like a healthy idle bot."),
    Q("core", "python.error-boundary",
      "Week 3 · A message arrives with an unknown type or a missing field. What should the receiving process do?",
      "Validate at the edge and fail at the message, not at the process: parse through the Pydantic discriminated union, and if validation fails, log the raw payload with the error and drop or reject that one message. An unknown type from a newer peer should be ignored rather than crash the reader, which is what makes adding a message type backward compatible. What you must never do is let a malformed message propagate as a half-filled object into the book or the portfolio."),
    Q("senior", "tools.protocol-contract",
      "Week 3 · You must add a field to a message that sixty bots in flight already parse. How do you change the wire without breaking anyone?",
      "Treat the schema as a versioned contract that only grows: add the field with a default so old senders still validate, never rename or remove a field, and make readers tolerant of fields and message types they do not know. Deploy readers before writers, so everyone can parse the new shape before anyone sends it, and add a round-trip test for the new model. A rename is a breaking change — in semantic-version terms a major — and on a live trading wire it is done as add-new, migrate everyone, then retire the old field much later."),

    # ---------------- Week 4: backtesting and data ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "quant.look-ahead",
      "Week 4 · What is look-ahead bias, and give two ways it sneaks into a backtest.",
      "It is using information at time t that was only available after t, which makes a strategy look better than anything that could trade. It sneaks in through a forward as-of join that attaches the next quote to a decision, through a feature computed on the whole series (a z-score using the full-sample mean), through a fill at the bar's close price when the decision was made from that close, or through reading the wall clock instead of the tape's logical clock. Each one is a clock violation, and each one has a test."),
    Q("core", "quant.event-driven-backtest",
      "Week 4 · List the ways a backtest lies, and what you report next to a backtest P&L so it means something.",
      "Look-ahead, survivorship (testing only on assets that still exist), snooping (trying many variants and reporting the best), free fills (trading at the mid with no fee), and ignored latency. A P&L alone means nothing: report it next to three baselines — do nothing, buy and hold, a naive rule — with the drawdown and a correctly defined Sharpe, and say how many variants you tried. A strategy that cannot beat buy-and-hold after costs is not a strategy."),
    Q("core", "quant.fill-simulation",
      "Week 4 · What makes a simulated fill honest?",
      "It walks the book rather than filling the whole quantity at the touch, it pays the spread and the taker fee (or earns the rebate only if a resting order would really have been hit), and it applies latency as a whole number of ticks between the decision and the fill, so the price can move in between. Passive fills need a queue-position assumption and are usually optimistic, so they deserve a pessimistic variant. A backtest whose fills are free is measuring the signal's dream, not its trade."),
    Q("core", "tools.headless-sim",
      "Week 4 · What is the logical clock, and how do you prove a backtest is deterministic?",
      "The logical clock is the tape's own ordering — tick and sequence number — as opposed to the real clock (time.time(), datetime.now()). Every decision in a replay must read the logical clock, so the result does not depend on the laptop's speed or the replay speed. The proof is a digest: hash the sequence of fills, run twice (and at two replay speeds), and the digests must match; any difference points at unseeded randomness, a wall-clock read, or unstable iteration order."),
    Q("core", "tools.column-store",
      "Week 4 · Why does a row store struggle with tick research, and what does a column store change?",
      "Research questions scan one or two fields over millions of rows — the mid of every AAPL tick in a month — and a row store must read every field of every row to do it. A column store keeps each field contiguous, so a scan reads only the columns it needs, compresses them well because neighbouring values are similar, and vectorises the computation. Parquet does this in a file with row groups and statistics for predicate pushdown; kdb+ and DuckDB do it as engines. A row store with an index still wins the point lookup of one order by id."),
    Q("senior", "tools.as-of-join",
      "Week 4 · Explain a backward and a forward as-of join, and which one is safe inside a backtest decision.",
      "An as-of join matches each left row to the most recent right row at or before its time (backward) or to the first one at or after it (forward), instead of requiring equal timestamps. Inside a decision only the backward join is safe, because it attaches the last quote you could have seen; a forward join attaches a future quote and is pure look-ahead. The forward join has honest uses after the fact — measuring the price some time after a fill for markouts or TCA — but never as an input to the decision."),

    # ---------------- Week 6: complexity ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "trading.price-time-priority",
      "Week 6 · How does the matching engine find the best price quickly and keep price-time priority exact?",
      "Each side keeps its price levels in a structure that exposes the best price in O(1) — a heap or a sorted container — and each level holds a FIFO queue of orders, so within a price the earliest order fills first. A dict from order id to order makes a cancel O(1) to find; the cancel is lazy, marking the order dead and skipping it when it reaches the front, instead of rescanning the book. A trade always executes at the resting order's price."),
    Q("core", "quant.rolling-stats",
      "Week 6 · Your on_tick recomputes a 200-tick standard deviation from scratch every tick. What do you change?",
      "Recomputing is O(window) per tick, multiplied by the whole session. Keep a deque(maxlen=200) and update running sums as values enter and leave, or use Welford's algorithm for the mean and variance, so each tick costs O(1). Prefer Welford-style updates to the sum-of-squares formula because subtracting two large, nearly equal numbers loses precision catastrophically; and check the O(1) version against the recomputed one in a test, with a tolerance you can defend."),
    Q("core", "tools.profiling",
      "Week 6 · How do you find out where on_tick spends its time, and name two classic benchmark mistakes.",
      "Measure before guessing: cProfile the whole run to find the hot functions, then timeit or perf_counter around the suspect block, and report percentiles rather than a mean. Classic mistakes are timing a single run (noise and warm-up dominate), timing on a toy input whose size hides the growth, measuring with the profiler on and quoting that number, and letting the optimiser of a native library or a cache make the benchmark measure nothing. Re-run with n doubled: the way the time grows tells you the complexity class."),
    Q("senior", "python.shortest-paths",
      "Week 6 · How is a currency or cross-venue arbitrage a negative cycle, and which algorithm finds it?",
      "Draw each asset as a node and each conversion as an edge weighted by -log(rate, after fees). A loop whose rates multiply to more than 1 has log-weights that sum to less than 0: a negative cycle. Dijkstra cannot handle negative weights, so you use Bellman-Ford, which relaxes every edge V-1 times and then detects a cycle if one more pass still improves a distance. In practice the cycle must survive fees, depth and latency, which is why most detected arbitrages disappear when you try to trade them."),
    Q("core", "quant.knapsack",
      "Week 6 · You have ten trade ideas, each with a capital need and an expected profit, and limited capital. What problem is this, and how do you solve it exactly?",
      "It is the 0/1 knapsack: pick a subset that maximises expected profit without exceeding capital. Greedy by profit-per-dollar is fast but can be wrong; dynamic programming over (idea, capital used) solves it exactly in O(n · C) once capital is discretised, by noticing that the best plan for the first k ideas and capital c reuses the best plans of smaller sub-problems. In practice the inputs are noisy estimates, so the exact optimum is less important than seeing that capital allocation is an optimisation with a budget constraint."),

    # ---------------- Week 7: big data ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "python.pandas-memory",
      "Week 7 · A tick DataFrame takes 30 MB. How do you cut it to about a quarter, and what do you give up?",
      "Measure first with memory_usage(deep=True), then downcast: float64 prices to float32 and int64 sizes to smaller integers, and repeated strings such as symbols to category, which stores each distinct value once plus small integer codes. That is how the Week 7 example went from about 30 MB to 7.5 MB. The cost is precision — float32 keeps about seven significant digits, enough for a price but not for summing a season of P&L — so keep accumulators in float64."),
    Q("core", "python.numpy-vectorize",
      "Week 7 · Why is a vectorised rolling z-score about forty times faster than a Python loop over rows, and how do you know it is still right?",
      "The loop pays interpreter overhead and object boxing for every element; the vectorised version (a grouped rolling transform in pandas, or numpy array operations) runs the same arithmetic in compiled code over contiguous memory, often with SIMD. The speed-up in the Week 7 exercise was about 40x. You prove it is right by computing both on the same data and asserting they agree within a tolerance you can defend, including the edges — the warm-up window and the boundaries between symbols."),
    Q("core", "python.chunking",
      "Week 7 · How do you compute a 50-tick rolling mean over a file too big to load, in bounded memory?",
      "Read it in chunks (pandas chunksize, or batches from Parquet), compute per chunk, and carry the state the window needs across the boundary: the last 49 values of each symbol, prepended to the next chunk before computing, then dropped from the output. Without that carry, the first rows of each chunk get a short window and the result differs from the in-memory version. Write a test that compares the chunked result with the full one on a file small enough to load."),
    Q("senior", "python.multiprocessing",
      "Week 7 · You spread a parameter sweep over an 8-core process pool and get a 5x speed-up. Why not 8x, and what makes the workers safe to retry?",
      "Each task pays to start, to pickle its arguments and results, and to load its data; the slowest task sets the finish time; and the cores share memory bandwidth and caches, so a memory-bound job scales worse than a compute-bound one. Amdahl's law then caps the speed-up by the serial part (loading, merging results). Workers are safe to retry when they are pure and idempotent — the output is a function of the inputs, written to a path keyed by them — so a crashed or duplicated task just recomputes the same file."),
    Q("core", "tools.parquet",
      "Week 7 · DuckDB on partitioned Parquet versus SQLite with an index: which wins a full-season scan, and which wins a single lookup?",
      "DuckDB on Parquet wins the scan: it reads only the needed columns, skips partitions and row groups by their statistics, and vectorises the aggregation. SQLite with an index wins the point lookup, because the B-tree goes straight to the row while a columnar file must at least locate and decode a row group. So the choice follows the question: analytics over many rows in columnar form, transactional lookups in a row store; many firms keep both."),

    # ---------------- Week 8: machine learning ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "quant.walk-forward",
      "Week 8 · Why does a shuffled train/test split lie on market data, and what do you do instead?",
      "Consecutive ticks are correlated, so a shuffled split puts near-identical neighbours in train and test and the model is graded on data it has effectively seen — the leakage gap, a fake accuracy that appears even on data with no signal. Instead, validate walk-forward: train on the past, leave a gap (purge and embargo) at each wall so labels that look ahead cannot overlap, test on the next block, and report the mean and the spread across folds."),
    Q("core", "quant.overfitting",
      "Week 8 · Your walk-forward result looks good. What control would tell you whether it is real?",
      "A shuffled-label control: rerun the identical pipeline with the labels randomly permuted, which destroys any real relationship. If the 'model' still scores well, the edge comes from leakage or snooping in the pipeline, not from the market. Also count how many variants you tried, since the best of many noisy results is biased upward, and check the result on a later period you have never looked at."),
    Q("core", "quant.signal-scoring",
      "Week 8 · A classifier predicts the next move with 55% accuracy. Does it make money?",
      "Not necessarily. Accuracy ignores the size of wins and losses and ignores costs: what matters is hit rate times average win against miss rate times average loss, minus the spread and fees paid on every trade, times how often it trades. A 55% signal that trades every tick can lose steadily to fees, while a 45% signal with large wins can be profitable. Report hit rate, payoff, turnover and P&L after costs, not accuracy alone."),
    Q("core", "quant.model-ops",
      "Week 8 · How does a trained model get into a live bot safely?",
      "Freeze it to a versioned artifact with the exact feature list and preprocessing it was trained with; load it once in __init__, never in on_tick; compute the features in on_tick from the same code used in training so there is no train/serve skew; predict; and fall back to doing nothing if the artifact is missing, a feature is NaN or the prediction raises. Then monitor it: one log line per interval with the prediction distribution and the live hit rate, so drift is visible before it costs money."),
    Q("senior", "quant.boosting",
      "Week 8 · Why start with a linear or logistic model before gradient boosting or a neural network?",
      "Because financial data is mostly noise, and a flexible model will memorise noise and report it as an edge. A simple model is a baseline that is fast, stable across folds and interpretable, so you can see whether a feature makes economic sense; regularisation keeps it honest. Climb the ladder — trees, forests, boosting, a small network — only when the out-of-sample, after-cost result improves by more than the spread across folds, and remember that each step adds parameters, tuning choices and therefore snooping risk."),
    Q("core", "tools.news-metadata",
      "Week 8 · What can go wrong when you turn news headlines into a trading feature?",
      "Timestamps are the first trap: use the time the item was available to you, not the event time or the time it was revised, or you have look-ahead. Duplicates and syndicated copies must be removed so one story does not count five times, and the mapping from headline to ticker must be checked. Then the sentiment score is just a feature: it goes through the same walk-forward validation and cost check as any other, and it usually decays within minutes."),

    # ---------------- Week 9: testing, git flow, CI/CD ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "tools.git-flow-branches",
      "Week 9 · What is each branch for in git flow: main, develop, feature, bugfix, release and hotfix?",
      "main is production: protected, every release tagged. develop integrates finished features. feature/<ticket> carries one change off develop and lives a few days; bugfix/<ticket> fixes unreleased work off develop. release/<version> freezes a version for final fixes and the version bump while develop moves on, then merges to main (tagged) and back to develop. hotfix/<ticket> starts from the live tag on main, is merged into main and tagged, and is merged back into develop so the fix survives the next release."),
    Q("warm-up", "tools.pytest",
      "Week 9 · How does pytest find your tests, and why can you use a plain assert?",
      "It collects files named test_*.py, functions named test_* and classes named Test*, and reports each test separately. It rewrites assert statements when it imports the test module, so a failure shows both sides of the comparison rather than a bare AssertionError, with no assertEqual boilerplate. Day to day: pytest -q for a summary, -k to filter by name, -x to stop at the first failure."),
    Q("core", "tools.pull-request-gate",
      "Week 9 · What must be true before a pull request may merge into main, and how is that enforced rather than remembered?",
      "A clear description (what, why, how it was tested), a linked ticket, two approvals including one from someone who did not write it, and all required checks green — lint, unit, integration, a coverage threshold, here also a seeded simulation run. It is enforced by branch protection: direct pushes to main are disabled and the merge button stays grey until the checks and approvals are in. Pick one merge strategy (squash, merge commit or rebase) and keep it, so the history stays readable."),
    Q("core", "tools.environments-release",
      "Week 9 · Describe the environment ladder from a laptop to production at a trading firm, and what staging must have to be worth anything.",
      "Local, then CI on a clean runner, then testing/QA, then staging, then a canary in production (one venue or one book, small size), then everything. Staging is worth something only if it is production-like: the same image and configuration, the same message schemas, replayed tapes or a shadow feed, ideally shadow trading next to the live system without sending orders. New behaviour ships behind a flag that defaults off, and rollback is a redeploy of the previous tag."),
    Q("core", "tools.change-window",
      "Week 9 · A bug in your live bot is found on Wednesday of a trading week. Walk through the fix.",
      "Branch hotfix/<ticket> from the tag that is live, not from develop, write the failing test that reproduces the bug first, fix it, and open a pull request with the required checks and two approvals. Merge it into main, tag a patch version (v1.3.0 to v1.3.1), and deploy in the change window — after the close or right after a graded block, never while a session is open — with the run-book open and the rollback tag written down. Then merge the hotfix back into develop so the next release does not reintroduce the bug, and add the incident to the postmortem log."),
    Q("core", "tools.pull-request-gate",
      "Week 9 · What do conventional commits and semantic versions buy you, and why is renaming a message field a major version?",
      "Conventional commits (feat:, fix:, chore:, feat!: for breaking) make the history machine-readable, so the next version and the CHANGELOG can be computed: a fix bumps the patch, a feature the minor, a breaking change the major. A version number is then a promise to every consumer about compatibility. Renaming a field on the wire breaks every bot that parses the old name, so it is a breaking change by definition, which is why a live wire only grows."),
    Q("senior", "tools.git-flow-branches",
      "Week 9 · Git flow, GitHub flow or trunk-based development: which would you choose for a four-person quant team, and which for a bank with four hundred developers?",
      "For the small team, trunk-based or GitHub flow: short-lived branches merged to a main that is always releasable, unfinished work hidden behind flags, and continuous deployment — release branches would only add ceremony. For the bank, git flow or a release-branch model fits: scheduled releases, several versions supported at once, regulators and change-approval boards that want a frozen release candidate. In both, trading adds the same layer on top: a change window, a two-person rule on order-sending code, and research separated from production."),
    Q("core", "tools.determinism",
      "Week 9 · A test passes twice and fails on the third run. Where do you look?",
      "At everything that is not a function of the inputs: unseeded randomness, a wall-clock read or uuid4() inside the logic, iteration order over a set or a dict keyed by objects with unstable hashes, state shared between tests, or a real network call. Seed the RNG, inject the clock and the id generator, use fixtures for fresh state, and replace the network with a fake or a recording. Until it is deterministic it is not a flake you may ignore; it is a bug in the test or in the code."),
    Q("core", "tools.mocking-async",
      "Week 9 · Your async test reports a pass but its assertion never executed. Why?",
      "Calling a coroutine function returns a coroutine object and runs nothing. Without @pytest.mark.asyncio (or asyncio_mode = auto in the configuration), pytest calls the function, gets that object, never awaits it, and records a pass with at most a warning. Mark every coroutine test, and make each new async test fail once on purpose to prove it really runs."),
    Q("core", "tools.test-pyramid",
      "Week 9 · You have 100% line coverage and still shipped a bug. What does coverage not tell you?",
      "Coverage proves a line executed, not that anything checked its result, and line coverage says nothing about which branch combinations ran — a suite of calls without asserts can score 100%. Use the report's Missing column to find code no test enters, and test the tests: break a line of production logic on purpose and see whether anything goes red. Set a high bar only where it is a design decision, like the matching engine, where a wrong fill corrupts every P&L downstream."),
    Q("core", "tools.docker",
      "Week 9 · Why pin dependencies with == rather than >=, and why does the order of Dockerfile layers matter?",
      "Pinning controls when you find out about upstream changes: with >= a new release can turn CI red on a commit that only touched a README, or break a bot at the open; with == the upgrade is a deliberate, tested commit. Docker caches each layer by its inputs, so put COPY requirements.txt and pip install above COPY . . — then a code change reuses the installed-dependency layer, and the image builds in seconds instead of reinstalling everything. Order layers from stable to volatile."),
    Q("senior", "tools.github-actions",
      "Week 9 · Your pipeline takes 25 minutes. What do you change, and what stays in the merge gate?",
      "At 25 minutes people stop waiting, then stop trusting it, then merge around it. Keep the gate fast and deterministic — lint, unit tests and one seeded, network-free end-to-end run — with cached dependencies, parallel jobs and fail-fast. Move long simulations, multi-seed sweeps and full image builds to a nightly or merge-queue job that reports rather than blocks. This platform's suite runs 2,036 tests in about 42 seconds, and that speed is a design goal, not an accident."),

    # ---------------- the season: market structure, risk, live ----------------
    Q("warm-up", "trading.primary-secondary",
      "Season · What happens in an IPO, from bookbuild to the first trade?",
      "The primary market raises capital: during the bookbuild investors submit demand (quantity, sometimes a price limit), the issuer and its bank set the offer price from that book, shares are allocated — pro rata when the deal is oversubscribed — and the security lists. On the first day the secondary market discovers the price, often away from the offer. In the arena the same lifecycle ran inside the season, with traders bidding through the on_ipo hook."),
    Q("core", "trading.multi-venue",
      "Season · Three venues show different asks and charge different taker fees. Where do you send a buy?",
      "To the lowest all-in price — the ask times one plus the taker fee — for the size you need, after checking depth, not to the best displayed quote. A venue with a slightly worse ask and a much lower fee is often cheaper, which is exactly how fee competition between student exchanges moved flow during the season. A real router also weighs fill probability, latency and the risk that the quote disappears before your order arrives."),
    Q("core", "trading.portfolio-pnl",
      "Season · Explain realised and unrealised P&L and net worth from a stream of fills.",
      "Keep cash, position and average entry price per symbol. Realised P&L is locked in when a position is reduced: (exit price - average entry) times the quantity closed, for a long. Unrealised P&L is the open position times (current mark - average entry), and net worth is cash plus the marked value of positions. Fees reduce cash on every fill and rebates increase it, which is why a strategy can be right on direction and still lose."),
    Q("core", "quant.risk-controls",
      "Season · Design a kill switch, and say how you would test it.",
      "Put it in front of the wire: before any order leaves, check realised plus unrealised P&L against a daily-loss limit, the position against a size limit and the margin against the requirement. On a breach it blocks all new orders, optionally sends one flatten, and latches — it stays tripped even if P&L recovers, until a human resets it. The test feeds synthetic fills until the limit is crossed and asserts that the next on_tick returns no order, that the flatten is sent once, and that a later recovery does not re-arm trading."),
    Q("core", "tools.failure-modes",
      "Season · The socket is healthy, the price looks plausible, but the feed has gone stale. How do you detect it and what should the bot do?",
      "Connection liveness is not data freshness, so track freshness directly: for each symbol, the tick or time at which the price last changed. Past a threshold the symbol is cold — stop quoting or widen sharply, do not open new positions, and log an anomaly. The same check catches a market maker that died without its quotes being cancelled, where the touch keeps answering with a price nobody stands behind."),
    Q("core", "trading.reconciliation",
      "Season · After a reconnect, which parts of your bot's state are trustworthy?",
      "What the venue sends is trustworthy because it overwrites yours: positions arrive on every portfolio update, so they self-heal within a tick. What you accumulated locally — realised P&L, fill counts, rolling statistics, the daily-loss tally behind your kill switch — is not, because your object was reset while the venue kept the truth. Re-sync from the venue's snapshot on rejoin, diff a fill-derived shadow ledger against the venue every tick, and treat any mismatch as an incident."),
    Q("senior", "quant.risk-adjusted-score",
      "Season · Design a season score that cannot be won by parking cash or by one maximally levered bet.",
      "Score risk-adjusted return, not final equity: per-snapshot returns minus the risk-free drift, so an idle book scores about zero; divided by the volatility of returns with a floor, so a flat curve cannot produce an astronomical ratio; multiplied by a drawdown penalty such as max(0, 1 - 2·maxDD), and clamped. Penalise by sign, so a deep drawdown makes a negative score worse rather than better. Rank within each role and announce the formula at the start, so it shapes behaviour. With equal +12.68% returns, the steady curve scores +10.000 and the jagged one +0.091."),
    Q("senior", "tools.runbook",
      "Season · What belongs in a run-book, and what makes a postmortem worth the hour?",
      "A run-book is one page per known failure — symptom, diagnosis, action — written calmly in advance and followed under pressure: 'no fills and retry lines in the log: check the venue, check the token, let the backoff run, re-sync the ledger on rejoin'. A postmortem is blameless and about the system: a timeline, a root cause found by asking why until it is specific, the impact, and fixes with owners. It is worth the hour only if the root cause is a missing check rather than a person, and if it ends as a regression test in the suite."),
    Q("core", "quant.tearsheet",
      "Season · Two bots end the season with the same net worth. Which do you hire, and what do you ask to see?",
      "The steadier one, but only after seeing the mechanism. Read the equity curve first, then volatility and Sharpe, max drawdown and where it happened, Calmar, turnover and the split between taker fees and maker rebates, hit rate with average win and loss, and the number of trades. Then ask for the thesis and whether the result survives another seed or another period: a good process can have a bad week and a bad process can be lucky once."),

    # ---------------- system design ----------------
    Q("senior", "tools.parquet",
      "System design · Design a market-data recorder for a venue that must be replayable bit for bit.",
      "Append every inbound and outbound message, as received, to a sequential log with a monotonic sequence number and both clocks (the venue's logical tick and a receive timestamp), one file per session, flushed in batches by a writer that never blocks the event loop. Make it bounded (a size cap with an alert) and integrity-checked (a running hash or per-file digest). Overnight, convert the logs to partitioned Parquet by date and symbol for research, keeping the raw log as the source of truth. The acceptance test is a replay through the headless simulator that reproduces the session's fill digest."),
    Q("senior", "tools.change-window",
      "System design · Design how a bot change is deployed during a trading week without risking the live sessions.",
      "The change is developed on a feature branch behind a flag that defaults off, merged through a pull request with required checks (unit, integration, a seeded golden run identical before and after) and two approvals, and released with a version tag. It is deployed to staging and shadow-run against a replayed tape, then deployed to production in the change window right after a session closes, still dark; the flag is turned on for one symbol or one venue first. The run-book names the metrics to watch and the rollback, which is a redeploy of the previous tag — and nothing is ever restarted while a session is open."),
    Q("senior", "python.containers",
      "System design · Design an order book that handles a million updates per second.",
      "Per symbol, one writer thread or core owns the book, so the hot path needs no locks; updates arrive through a lock-free queue. Prices are integer ticks, so each side can be an array indexed by price offset (O(1) update and a cached best-price index) or a tree of levels for wide ranges; each level is a FIFO of orders and a hash map from order id gives O(1) cancel and modify. Memory is preallocated in pools, nothing allocates or rescans on the hot path, and you measure p99 and p99.9 latency under a replayed peak load. At this rate the implementation is C++ or similar, not Python — the design is the same one the arena's book uses, with the constants removed."),
    Q("senior", "quant.look-ahead",
      "System design · Design a research platform in which it is hard to leak the future by accident.",
      "Make time a first-class input: every dataset carries the time at which each row became available, and every query is made as of a decision time through a backward as-of join by default. Features are computed by one library shared between research and production, so there is no second implementation to diverge; splits are walk-forward with purge and embargo by construction; and every experiment is logged with its data version, code commit and the number of variants tried. The platform also runs the controls automatically — a shuffled-label run and a baseline — next to every reported result."),
    Q("senior", "quant.risk-controls",
      "System design · Design the risk layer between a strategy's signal and the wire.",
      "Every order passes one pre-trade gate, in the order-sending process, that no strategy can bypass: fat-finger checks on price and size, position and notional limits per symbol and in total, a daily-loss check against real-time P&L, a margin check and a message-rate limit. The kill switch sits in the same layer and latches; it can be tripped automatically or by a human, and it cancels resting orders. The layer is deterministic and heavily tested, it fails closed (if the state is unknown, it rejects), every rejection is logged with its reason, and a two-person rule applies to any change in it."),
    Q("senior", "quant.model-ops",
      "System design · Design the service that runs an ML signal in production.",
      "The model is a versioned artifact in a registry with its training data window, feature list, metrics and code commit. Features are computed online by the same code used offline, from the live book, with as-of semantics; the model is loaded at start-up, not per request, and inference has a latency budget. Every failure path — a missing artifact, a NaN feature, a timeout — returns 'no view' so the strategy stays flat rather than trading on garbage. Monitoring tracks the input distributions, the prediction distribution and the live hit rate against the walk-forward estimate, and a new model version goes through shadow mode and a canary before it trades."),
]
